<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Emirate of Chefchaouen</h1><p class="st-meta">1471 CE – 1561 CE (approx.) · capital Chefchaouen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A mountain emirate in the Rif founded as a base for holy war against Portuguese Ceuta, a refuge for exiles from Granada.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chefchaouen">Chefchaouen</a> was founded in 1471 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-rashid">Ali ibn Rashid</a>, a sharif of the Banu Rashid, as a fortified base for raids against the Portuguese enclaves of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-morocco">Portuguese Morocco</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ksar-es-seghir">Ksar es-Seghir</a>. Muslim and Jewish refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> swelled the town after 1492.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> ruled the western Rif and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jbala">Jbala</a> hills semi-independently, nominally recognising the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/wattasid-sultanate">Wattasid Sultanate</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a>. Andalusi settlers gave the town its whitewashed houses and craft traditions. Ali's daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayyida-al-hurra">Sayyida al-Hurra</a> married the lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetouan">Tetouan</a> and later governed that city herself, linking the two states.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saadi-sultanate">Saadi Sultanate</a> subdued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> around 1561, ending Banu Rashid rule. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chefchaouen">Chefchaouen</a> remained a closed holy town, rarely visited by Europeans before the 20th century, and is today a well-known Moroccan city.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1471 CE – 1561 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:88.98%;width:1.79%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1471 CE – 1561 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chefchaouen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ali ibn Rashid</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Berber</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Banu Rashid, Emirate of Chaouen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1516&amp;focus=emirate-of-chefchaouen" aria-label="Emirate of Chefchaouen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4547 906 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Emirate of Chefchaouen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4867 1162L4873 1200M4867 1162L4873 1200"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4873 1200h0M4873 1200h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4867 1162h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4867 1162h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chefchaouen. <a href="/atlas?year=1516&amp;focus=emirate-of-chefchaouen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

